---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import lockup from '../assets/brand/nendo.png';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

// Quoted from docs/vision.md. These are load-bearing: a change that violates one
// needs an accepted ADR, so they are reproduced rather than paraphrased.
const axioms = [
  { title: 'Empty files are valid', body: 'A .nendo file with no user schema is a working application, not an error state.' },
  { title: 'Studio is permanent and host-owned', body: 'No application content and no agent can remove the route back to the data.' },
  { title: 'Data is usable before specialised presentation', body: 'The table works before the form exists, and after it breaks.' },
  { title: 'All clients share one authority and validation path', body: 'Native UI, web Workbench and MCP adapter call the same typed application services.' },
  { title: 'Agents never receive raw SQL or filesystem authority', body: 'No SQL, no SQLite handle, no database path, no generic host invocation.' },
  { title: 'Preview is physically separate from active state', body: 'Validation happens on a clone with no authority over the active file.' },
  { title: 'History and reversibility claims are explicit', body: 'Every operation declares its reversibility class. There is no universal undo.' },
  { title: 'Local and offline use never depends on an agent or a Nendo service', body: 'The file opens, and the application works, with nothing connected.' },
];

const references = [
  { name: 'Idea Garden', role: 'The original shape', body: 'The first form and board, and the journey a newcomer is handed: empty file, human fallback, agent-authored shape, CSV load, focused surfaces, offline recovery.' },
  { name: 'Decision Log', role: 'The neutrality proof', body: 'Built to show the authoring path is not Idea-specific. The same vocabulary produces a different application.' },
  { name: 'Axiom Register', role: 'Built over MCP alone', body: 'Record pages, related lists, declared filters, summary tiles and multi-step commands — authored from an empty file through the MCP interface, reading the vocabulary off the wire.' },
  { name: 'Nendo Station', role: 'The demonstration', body: 'A fictional habitat run as an operations room: a front page, record pages with tabs, a pinned schematic custom view, commands, a trigger that fires, and an agent change reviewed in front of you.' },
];

const inScope = [
  'Empty-file lifecycle', 'Scalar schema and relational data', 'Studio table and editor',
  'CSV import and export', 'Semantic forms, lists, boards, record pages',
  'Related lists, declared filters, summary tiles', 'Declarative commands',
  'MCP inspection and authoring', 'Proposal preview and promotion',
  'History and bounded compensation', 'Safe mode',
  'Bounded calculations and local actions',
];

const outOfScope = [
  'General scripting and expressions', 'Plug-ins and third-party controls',
  'An embedded agent', 'Scalar multi-choice', 'Binary and asset fields',
  'Collaboration and cloud sync', 'Background agents', 'Other database engines',
  'Cross-platform parity',
];
---

<Page
  title="Concept and philosophy — Nendo"
  description="Why Nendo exists: the problem with generated applications, the clay proposition, the eight product axioms, and the single result that would falsify the whole idea."
  eyebrow="Concept and philosophy"
  heading="Clay, not a build output."
  lede="Nendo is a bet that an application can be a thing you reshape rather than a thing you regenerate — and it is written so that the bet can lose."
>
  <section class="page section">
    <Prose>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        AI can generate a small application in minutes. Each one becomes another silo:
        the data is coupled to disposable code, every change means regeneration, and the
        user has no visibility into schema migration, history or recovery. When the
        generated code stops working, the data goes with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Low-code platforms solve part of this and introduce their own version of it. They
        require a hosted platform and proprietary storage. They are not a durable local
        file you can copy, inspect and reopen offline in ten years.
      </p>

      <h2>The proposition</h2>
      <p>
        Software should behave like structured clay. One portable SQLite file — the
        <code>.nendo</code> file — holds durable identity, schema, data, application
        meaning and history. People and coding agents reshape it in place through typed
        semantic operations. No generated project, no build step, no regeneration.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Nendo</em> (粘土) is Japanese for clay, and the name is the thesis rather than
        a decoration. Clay keeps a shape and takes a new one without being thrown away.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <p class="eyebrow">The axioms</p>
    <h2>Eight load-bearing refusals.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      These are quoted from <a href={`${base}/docs/vision`}>the vision document</a>
      as written. A change that violates one needs an accepted decision record, not a
      good reason — which is what makes them worth stating in public.
    </p>
    <ol class="axioms">
      {axioms.map((axiom, index) => (
        <li>
          <span class="axiom-number">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div>
            <h3>{axiom.title}</h3>
            <p>{axiom.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <div class="panel falsify">
      <p class="eyebrow">What would falsify this</p>
      <h2>An application whose shape nobody anticipated.</h2>
      <p>
        The proof is that an agent, given only the MCP interface, can build an application
        whose shape nobody anticipated — and that a human can review that change and
        understand what it does before accepting it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The negative case is stated just as plainly. If Studio succeeds but semantic
        surfaces cannot deliver value the table alone does not, Nendo has not passed its
        hypothesis. That is a pivot-or-stop signal, not a backlog item.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <p class="eyebrow">The evidence so far</p>
    <h2>Four reference applications, shaped differently on purpose.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      One application proves a path exists. Four differently shaped ones start to show the
      path is not specific to the first thing built on it. The last two were built from an
      empty file through the MCP interface alone.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-2 references">
      {references.map(item => (
        <article class="panel">
          <p class="reference-role">{item.role}</p>
          <h3>{item.name}</h3>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Scope</p>
    <h2>What is in, and what is deliberately out.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The out list is not a roadmap. Each entry is a decision, and several of them exist
      so that the things on the in list can be small enough to reason about.
    </p>
    <div class="scope">
      <div>
        <h3>In</h3>
        <ul class="chips">{inScope.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Out, without an accepted decision record</h3>
        <ul class="chips chips-out">{outOfScope.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <div class="closing">
      <Image src={lockup} alt="Nendo" width={210} densities={[1, 2]} />
      <div>
        <h2>The success criterion, in one sentence.</h2>
        <p>
          A new user creates an empty file, shapes or imports a modest dataset, uses it
          productively through Studio, has an external agent build a materially better
          focused surface, reviews and accepts the semantic change, restarts offline, and
          recovers the data without that surface.
        </p>
        <p><a href={`${base}/how-it-works`}>How the machinery behind that works →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .axioms {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 28px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(330px, 1fr));
  }

  .axioms li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 20px 22px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
  }

  .axiom-number {
    flex: none;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--violet);
    padding-top: 2px;
  }

  .axioms h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 5px; }
  .axioms p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.93rem; }

  .falsify h2 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .falsify p { max-width: 62ch; }

  .references { margin-top: 26px; }
  .references p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.93rem; }
  .references h3 { margin-bottom: 8px; }

  .reference-role {
    margin: 0 0 4px;
    color: var(--cobalt);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    font-weight: 720;
    letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .scope {
    display: grid;
    gap: 30px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    margin-top: 26px;
  }

  .chips {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .chips li {
    padding: 6px 13px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
  }

  .chips-out li {
    color: var(--muted);
    background: var(--surface-soft);
    text-decoration: line-through;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--danger) 50%, transparent);
  }

  .closing {
    display: grid;
    gap: 30px;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(280px, 1fr);
    align-items: center;
    padding: clamp(24px, 4vw, 40px);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .closing p { max-width: 58ch; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .closing { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
